{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"6740111cf72033c4","name":"card_event_flight_to_oblivion","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/WorldAtWar/events/card_event_flight_to_oblivion","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"FLIGHT TO OBLIVION","text":"Remove the top air unit of your deck. Distribute damage randomly to enemies equal to its attack.","flavorText":"In the final months of WWII, Japan launched increasingly desperate kamikaze attacks against advancing Allied forces. By late 1944, Japan’s conventional military strength had been badly eroded, leading to the organized use of suicide tactics through units like the Tokubetsu Kōgekitai, or Special Attack Units. These missions were often carried out by young pilots flying outdated or lightly armed aircraft packed with explosives. The Battle of Okinawa in 1945 marked the peak of kamikaze activity. Japan launched more than 1,500 kamikaze aircraft during the campaign, sinking or damaging over 300 US and Allied ships. Some of these aircraft included the purpose-built Yokosuka MXY-7 Ohka, a rocket-powered bomb piloted by a single man, which was carried close to target areas by larger bombers. Many of these attacks were launched in the literal eleventh hour of Japan's war effort, when fuel, aircraft, and trained pilots were in critical short supply. Despite their tactical impact, the kamikaze missions could not alter the outcome of the war. These final strikes reflected the extent of Japan’s resolve and the dire strategic situation it faced in 1945.","faction":"Japan","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_flight_to_oblivion","title":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_flight_to_oblivion_title","SourceString":"FLIGHT TO OBLIVION","LocalizedString":"FLIGHT TO OBLIVION"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Japan","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_flight_to_oblivion_text","SourceString":"Remove the top air unit of your deck. Distribute damage randomly to enemies equal to its attack.","LocalizedString":"Remove the top air unit of your deck. Distribute damage randomly to enemies equal to its attack."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_flight_to_oblivion_history","SourceString":"In the final months of WWII, Japan launched increasingly desperate kamikaze attacks against advancing Allied forces. By late 1944, Japan’s conventional military strength had been badly eroded, leading to the organized use of suicide tactics through units like the Tokubetsu Kōgekitai, or Special Attack Units. These missions were often carried out by young pilots flying outdated or lightly armed aircraft packed with explosives. The Battle of Okinawa in 1945 marked the peak of kamikaze activity. Japan launched more than 1,500 kamikaze aircraft during the campaign, sinking or damaging over 300 US and Allied ships. Some of these aircraft included the purpose-built Yokosuka MXY-7 Ohka, a rocket-powered bomb piloted by a single man, which was carried close to target areas by larger bombers. Many of these attacks were launched in the literal eleventh hour of Japan's war effort, when fuel, aircraft, and trained pilots were in critical short supply. Despite their tactical impact, the kamikaze missions could not alter the outcome of the war. These final strikes reflected the extent of Japan’s resolve and the dire strategic situation it faced in 1945.","LocalizedString":"In the final months of WWII, Japan launched increasingly desperate kamikaze attacks against advancing Allied forces. By late 1944, Japan’s conventional military strength had been badly eroded, leading to the organized use of suicide tactics through units like the Tokubetsu Kōgekitai, or Special Attack Units. These missions were often carried out by young pilots flying outdated or lightly armed aircraft packed with explosives. The Battle of Okinawa in 1945 marked the peak of kamikaze activity. Japan launched more than 1,500 kamikaze aircraft during the campaign, sinking or damaging over 300 US and Allied ships. Some of these aircraft included the purpose-built Yokosuka MXY-7 Ohka, a rocket-powered bomb piloted by a single man, which was carried close to target areas by larger bombers. Many of these attacks were launched in the literal eleventh hour of Japan's war effort, when fuel, aircraft, and trained pilots were in critical short supply. Despite their tactical impact, the kamikaze missions could not alter the outcome of the war. These final strikes reflected the extent of Japan’s resolve and the dire strategic situation it faced in 1945."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Japan/World_at_War/T_flight_to_oblivion.T_flight_to_oblivion","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/ELEVENTH_HOUR.ELEVENTH_HOUR","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":401.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_flight_to_oblivion_photo","SourceString":"A Japanese Mitsubishi A6M Zero kamikaze moments before it struck the light cruiser USS Columbia on 6 January 1945.","LocalizedString":"A Japanese Mitsubishi A6M Zero kamikaze moments before it struck the light cruiser USS Columbia on 6 January 1945."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"isRemoval":true},"variables":[{"name":"attackValue","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"damageToDeal","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"unitFound","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"count","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_flight_to_oblivion","